Patch 2069 (fix line): pipelines.bin is read with 64-bit offsets, never appends a key it already holds, and is compacted.

Symptom: after a long time of play the pipeline warm-up (patch 2068) did nothing. The reader took the size of pipelines.bin
with `long ftell`, which is 32 bits on Windows: at 2 GiB or more the size reads as zero or negative, the buffer stayed NULL and
the loader returned with no log line, so the warm table stayed empty for the whole session. Worse, the noted-set that stops a
pipeline being appended twice is filled from that same read, so with the read failing every pipeline of every run was appended
again: the file grew by a whole table (1.5 to 2.25 GB) per start (4.35 and 4.50 GB were seen). A correct file can reach about
2.26 GB by itself (16384 records, mean 137.7 KB), so the 2^31 line is crossed at about 15,590 records.

New libs/video/psow_file.h (plain C, host-tested by installer/tools/psow_file_test.c):
 - every size and offset is 64-bit (_ftelli64/_fseeki64 on Windows, ftello/fseeko elsewhere); the same in shader_cache.bin's
   loader (sc_open), which used `long good = ftell(in)`.
 - every way out of the loader logs one [PSOW] line with the reason and the size; success logs the byte size, the record
   count and how many were repeats, over capacity or a damaged tail.
 - the file is scanned once, seeking over the bytecode (one small read per record, so scanning a 4.5 GB file needs no memory); when it
   has repeated keys, a damaged tail (a torn record would hide everything appended after it), more records than the table
   holds, or no file head, it is written again to pipelines.bin.tmp (first record of each key, at most 16384) and replaces the
   old file in one step (MoveFileEx REPLACE_EXISTING|WRITE_THROUGH on Windows, rename elsewhere) after flush and sync. When any
   step fails the temporary file is removed, the old file is kept, and warm-up is off for the run with a log line.
 - a file head {PSWH, format, stamp, record head size} records the build. Records are keyed by a hash of the translated bytecode,
   so records of an older translator are never hit again, yet were built at every start and filled the 16384 slots until new
   pipelines were no longer kept. A head from another build empties the file. A file without a head (written before this
   patch) is kept (deduplicated, given a head). The stamp is made by the port's CMake (jsp_psow_stamp in jsp_build_id.cmake) as
   a hash of the content of the video translators, the D3D12 backend and psow_file.h and passed as PSOW_BUILD_STAMP: it does not
   change when the exe is built again, moved or built with another toolchain (the exe is built on each player's PC). Without the
   define (a build outside that CMake) the build time of the backend file stands in, and the log says which is in use. The
   d3dcompiler version is not part of it.
 - a record is checked against its key (the key is a hash of VS, PS and the stored description): a rewrite leaves out a record
   that no longer hashes to it (the old code appended after a torn record, so a torn record could sit in the middle of a file
   and swallow the next ones), the warm-up skips and counts one that fails at read time, and psow_note refuses to file a
   record that does not reproduce its key. Limit: the scan cannot resynchronise after a torn record, so the records behind it
   in such an older file are lost, not misread.
 - memory: the warm-up no longer holds the file (up to 2.3 GB) in RAM. The scan notes the offset of each record and each warm-up
   thread reads one record at a time from its own handle. (A rewrite of an older file reads and hashes every kept record once.)
 - the capacity is a real limit: PSOW_SLOTS 32768 keys at half load = 16384 records (as before). A full file says so once. It keeps
   the first 16384 pipelines it was given and refuses new ones until the stamp changes (no eviction).
 - psow_note loads the file first (psow_start under the lock), so a pipeline built before the first frame's psow_start cannot
   be appended a second time; the offset list is freed when the last warm-up build is done, and the frames after the start
   do not take the lock.
 - when the warm-up refuses a record (it no longer hashes to its key, or its input-layout count disagrees with the stored description)
   it forgets the key, so the pipeline can be noted again, and leaves pipelines.bin.rewrite so the next start rewrites the file.
 - an I/O error on a record head is an error (old file kept, warm-up off for the run), not the end of the list; a failed replace
   logs GetLastError() and a temporary file that cannot be removed is reported.

Not tested on the GPU or under Wine: the host test (installer/tools/psow_file_test.c) covers the file logic with a sparse file over 2 GiB.

